<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Vanderbilt “<a href="https://cdn.vanderbilt.edu/vu-wpfsx/wp-content/uploads/sites/51/2026/06/State-of-Scholarship-Report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Report on the State of Scholarship in the Humanities and the Humanistic Social Sciences</a>,” was published this past summer, with a <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/10/27/the-vanderbilt-reports-sub-report-on-philosophy/">special report about academic philosophy</a> authored by <a href="https://as.nyu.edu/faculty/kit-fine.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kit Fine</a> (NYU) and <a href="https://grosen.scholar.princeton.edu/">Gideon Rosen</a> (Princeton), released last month. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In the following guest post, <a href="https://www.taliamaebettcher.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Talia Bettcher</a> (Eddie Goldenberg Research Chair of Canada in Gender-Based Violence and Trans Community Resilience, University of Waterloo) and <a href="https://www.jennifermathersaul.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Jennifer Saul</a> (Canada Research Chair in the Study of Hatred, Misinformation and Communication, University of Waterloo) respond to Fine and Rosen&#8217;s report. </p>



